Visual plan: the flow_clips are the shots of one short cinematic film about the machine, in the order of the
beats: Studio = the machine alone on a dark set; Cutaway = its casing or hull opens along a clean cut to show the real
inner parts working; Flow = glowing light traces show how electricity (cyan), heat (amber) or fluid and air (blue)
move through the real parts; In situ = the machine where it is mounted, on a vehicle or in a plant that stands still
(the engine pod under a parked airliner's wing at dusk, the alternator in an open engine bay, a crane on a quiet building
site), as a tight cinematic close-up with mood (runway lights, haze, reflections) where the machine fills the frame.
The vehicle never flies, drives or dives in a shot.
- Frame every shot like a product film: the machine fills at least half of the frame in every camera angle. Never a
  whole vehicle in a big landscape, never a wide establishing shot: the AI video model loses track of where the parts
  of a big scene are (it drew an engine away from its wing and a submarine that surfaced mid-shot).
- hero_look ({{ hero_look_words[0] }}-{{ hero_look_words[1] }} words): exactly how this machine looks, written once and repeated in every clip
  so the AI video model draws the same machine every time: a real, typical example (type and size), its shape,
  materials and colours. Only the machine itself, never a place, a pedestal or a background: each scene names its own
  place, and a place in the hero_look would contradict it. Never a fantasy design. The finish is brand-new and
  flawless, like a premium product film: smooth, polished surfaces with crisp reflections; no rust, dirt, stains,
  salt, weathering or wear.
  When the topic is a system inside a bigger machine (a cooling circuit, a gearbox), the hero is the whole machine
  (the engine, the car) and the cutaway shots go inside it. A pedestal is only for a part small enough to carry.
- The film is a story told in styles. Every flow_clip has a style, which fixes its camera:
  - product: the whole machine on a dark set, three smooth camera angles (the opening "look at it" shot);
  - open: the casing opens to show the inside; the camera is almost still, one slow small move;
  - explain: how it works, the camera locked and still. When air, fuel, fluid or exhaust flows through the
    machine, it stands upright as a clean vertical cutaway, intake at the top and outlet at the bottom, and the flow
    runs only from top to bottom (cool blue at the top changing slowly through amber to red heat at the bottom,
    where heat is involved). A part that only passes on turning force (a gearbox, a differential, a brake) has no
    intake or outlet: show it as a clean cutaway from the side, every part in its real place, moving as it really
    does;
  - close: the casing closes again; almost still, one slow small move;
  - installed: the machine where it is mounted, on a vehicle that stands still; two or three gentle angles;
  - moving: the vehicle moves, but the camera is fixed to it and points at the part, so the part stays still and
    large in the frame and only the surroundings move past.
  A typical story: product or open, then explain, then close or installed, then moving. Shorts use 3 of them.
- every flow_clip has:
  - style: one of product, open, explain, close, installed, moving.
  - start_picture: the first frame in a few words (e.g. "the closed engine seen from below on a dark set").
  - end_frame: the last frame in a few words (e.g. "the engine with its casing open"), not "The last frame shows...".
  - continues: true when the clip starts exactly where the previous flow_clip ended (its start picture is that
    end_frame); false when the story cuts to a new view. Chained clips keep the machine identical between shots.
  - scene (40-80 words): what happens between the start and end picture, in concrete physical detail, with real
    parts and real proportions, in exactly one place named precisely ("on a dark studio set", "in an open engine
    bay"). The place never changes inside a clip. The first flow_clip opens on motion.
  - camera: for product and installed, the smooth angles with lenses (e.g. "opens on a wide shot on a 35 mm lens,
    glides into a low orbit, finishes on a close-up of the pulley on an 85 mm lens"); for the other styles a short
    note, since their camera rule is fixed. Every angle stays close to the machine.
- No invented physics: parts never swap ends (no exhaust or outlet at the intake), never appear, disappear, float
  away or change shape; a machine keeps one shape from the first frame to the last; nothing changes place in a shot
  (nothing dives and then surfaces); flows go one way only. The AI video model invents such things when the camera
  roams or the shot asks for too much, so keep each clip to one clear change between its start and end picture.
- Open and close shots show the machine from the side or from below, cut open along its length; explain shots
  of a machine something flows through stand it upright so every flow runs top to bottom. A flow names its screen direction ("air enters on the left and leaves on the right")
  and moves only that way: nothing flows backwards or out of the wrong opening, and flames or hot gas appear only
  where they really are. In these shots the camera never looks head-on into an inlet or outlet: from the front the
  AI video model cannot tell in from out (it shot a jet engine's air and fire out of the intake).
- Spinning parts (rotors, shafts, turbine and compressor wheels, fans) turn in smooth slow motion, so every blade,
  coil and edge stays sharp and detailed: in the channel's past videos the spinning parts came out soft and blurry.
- Never military: show a civilian, research or commercial version (no weapons, no navy markings or warship look).
- No text, numbers, logos, brand names, flags, people, hands or faces in any shot.
